WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.086
Local interactive inference has a different personality than server tuning.

2
00:00:05.086 --> 00:00:10.866
The user is usually waiting directly in front of the machine, so responsiveness matters.

3
00:00:10.866 --> 00:00:23.350
A coding assistant, research helper, or document analysis workflow may tolerate modest total response time, but it feels broken if the first token takes too long or generation stutters badly.

4
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:28.667
For this environment, conservative settings are often the right starting point.

5
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:36.913
Choose a batch size and microbatch size that leave memory headroom for the operating system, editor, browser, and other tools.

6
00:00:36.913 --> 00:00:43.309
If the workload uses long-context prompts, test with realistic documents instead of short demos.

7
00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:49.011
Long prompts can make a configuration that looked stable suddenly feel slow or fragile.

8
00:00:49.011 --> 00:00:52.248
Balance prompt speed against smooth generation.

9
00:00:52.248 --> 00:01:03.422
A larger batch setting may speed up ingestion of a long prompt, but if it causes memory pressure, thermal throttling, or uneven generation, the user experience may be worse.

10
00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:12.746
A smaller setting that streams reliably can be better for interactive work than a larger setting that wins one benchmark and then causes pauses.

11
00:01:12.746 --> 00:01:17.986
Document stable settings by model, quantization, context length, and runtime.

12
00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:24.383
A 7B model, a 13B model, and a larger mixture model can behave very differently on the same hardware.

13
00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:28.005
Treat each configuration as its own operating profile.

14
00:01:28.005 --> 00:01:36.250
The result should be boring in the best way: predictable startup, predictable memory use, and predictable response behavior.

15
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.720
Local users also notice interruptions that aggregate metrics hide.

16
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:49.274
A benchmark may report an acceptable average, while the actual assistant pauses at awkward times or makes the editor feel sluggish.

17
00:01:49.274 --> 00:02:00.139
Treat subjective responsiveness as evidence, then connect it back to measurements such as first-token latency, memory headroom, CPU load, and sustained token rate.
